<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">W. B. Yeats</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1865">1865</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as William Butler Yeats</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">W. B. Yeats was an Irish poet and dramatist, a leading figure of the Irish literary revival and of modernist poetry. He co-founded the Abbey <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, served as a senator of the Irish Free State and received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1923; his poems include Easter, 1916 and The Second Coming.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/w-b-yeats.jpg" alt="W. B. Yeats" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Charles Beresford · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Butler_Yeats_by_George_Charles_Beresford.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Writers including Jane Austen, Charles Dickens, the Brontë sisters, George Eliot, W.B. <b>Yeats</b> and James Joyce made the period a high point of literature in English.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In literature it is often summed up in <b>W. B. Yeats</b>’s line “Things fall apart; the centre cannot hold”: modernists searched for a metaphysical centre and experienced its collapse.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Writers as diverse as Henry Fielding and Naguib Mahfouz allude to the Nights by name, and among those it influenced are Jorge Luis Borges, Italo Calvino, Angela Carter, Gustave Flaubert, Goethe, Victor Hugo, Marcel Proust, Alexander Pushkin, Salman Rushdie, Leo Tolstoy and <b>W. B. Yeats</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
